  "query_preview": "..\nThis afternoon through Sunday: The region will be transitioning \nto a milder, breezier and progressive pattern. Weak impulses \nsliding by the Canadian border will bring limited shower and \nt-storms chances this afternoon and evening and even more isolated\nrisk Saturday afternoon. Otherwise look for variable cloud cover,\nwith periods of middle to high clouds. Temperatures will be mild \nover the next couple days, with highs in the 70s and 80s, with \nsome ares pushing toward 90 in the deeper basin. Winds will be \nbreezy over the next couple days, especially in the afternoon and \nevening. Speeds of 10-20 mph with gusts of 20-40 mph, highest near\nthe Cascades to western Basin. Elevated fire weather conditions \nare possible with the combination of hot, dry and breezy \nconditions, with a risk for fires escaping in such conditions \nespecially in grassy areas.\n\nSunday night to Friday: Ensembles are trying to come together in \nthis period, showing a deep low approaching the area Sunday night \nand slipping in Monday into Tuesday before sagging south \nthereafter. This will bring cooler temperatures and increasing \nprecipitation chances. Sunday night into Monday much of the rain \nthreat will be in the Cascades, becoming more likely there by late\nMonday morning to afternoon. Then chances expand east late Monday\nafternoon into Monday night, when PoPs will be highest area-wide.\nSome afternoon thunderstorms are possible, with the best risk \nMonday afternoon and evening. Precipitation amounts in general \nwill not be too heavy, but locally higher amounts are possible in \nt-storms. Monday will remain mild, with highs in the 70s to low \n80s, with areas near the Cascades being coolest. Some high \nmountain snow is possible Monday night into Tuesday near the \nCascade crest, so those heading in the higher terrain this holiday\nweekend should be prepared. Tuesday will be the coolest day, with\nhighs in the upper 50s and 60s, with some areas near 70 in the \ndeeper basin. Upper 60s and 70s are forecast beyond. Wind will \nremain breezy to windy, with Monday seeing the strongest winds of \n10-25 mph and gusts of 20-40 mph. The strongest of these are \nforecast near the Cascades and from the Okanogan Valley south into\nColumbia Basin. /Solveig",
